  "summary": "I found 18 rallies in your match and made a reel of your 5 best. Your longest rally was 17 shots, from about 0:54 to 1:06. For your forehand, I made a breakdown video with 3 coaching cards, plus a video comparing you with the pros whose build and form are closest to yours: Fan Zhendong, Sun Yingsha and Tomokazu Harimoto.\n\n**Fix these first:**\n\n1. **Give your arm more room at contact.** Your elbow stays tucked low against your side, so you lift the bat by bending your arm instead. At contact your elbow is about 23\u00b0 more bent than Fan Zhendong's, and your upper arm sits about 20\u00b0 lower than his and Sun Yingsha's. Lift your elbow a little and hit the ball further out from your hip. That gives you a faster, looser swing.\n2. **Finish high.** Your bat is moving slightly downward through the ball, and your swing stops short. Brush up and forward so the bat ends near your forehead. That makes the stroke a real drive rather than a short hit.\n3. **Push with your legs.** You start with good knee bend but stand up straight by the time you hit. Stay low and push up into the ball. Then your legs supply the power and your arm doesn't have to work as hard.\n\n**Drill:** Do 3 sets of 20 shadow swings in front of a mirror. On each one, push from your legs, hit with your arm away from your hip, and hold the bat still by your forehead at the end.\n\nThese measurements come from only one forehand. The camera angle also made your body shape hard to measure, so the order of the 3 pros isn't certain. The two arm problems are the most reliable findings, especially the low elbow, which shows up against two of the pros. For a sharper comparison next time, film yourself from the side and tell me your height.",
